Part 2 of 4 of component-level sustainability diagnosis: **component inventory and performance evidence**. It is stacked on [#32](https://github.com/mgifford/firefox-energy-scanner/pull/32), with that PR's branch as its base, so the diff shows only this step. Retarget to `main` once #32 merges.

A scan now records which components its pages are built from, which ones recur, what each holds, and which Lighthouse findings fall inside each one. **No component is given an energy figure.** Firefox's power counters cover whole processes and its profile has no DOM references. A component's effect on energy is only ever a measured difference between two runs (PR 4).

## Existing capabilities reused

- **`collectPageAnatomy`**: the same DOM walk after the measured window, extended from PR 1's signatures to individual occurrences. No extra visit.
- **Lighthouse 13**: already optional and tested. Its insight audits derive layout shifts, non-composited animations, the LCP element, DOM-size nodes, forced reflows, long tasks, script CPU and third-party time from a **Chromium trace**. This PR keeps those findings instead of building a second trace pipeline. Lighthouse identifies elements by DevTools node path, so Firefox records the same path; the format is copied from Lighthouse's own `getNodePath`.
- **`diagnose`** and **`collectTwigTemplates`**: Twig regions now also record where each template's output starts, so diagnose can name the template behind each component (development only).
- **Page families**, **stats**, **report conventions** and the **site index** from PR 1.

## New code

- `ComponentInstance` in `page-anatomy.ts`. Up to 24 per page: the largest, at most 3 per signature, then those holding media or animations. Each one records:
  - selector, node path and ancestry;
  - ARIA role and accessible name;
  - nodes, depth and position (whether it starts below the first screen);
  - images without lazy loading below the first screen, autoplay video, iframes, canvases;
  - resources and Web Animations (endless ones included);
  - live regions.
- `src/lighthouse/evidence.ts`: element findings, at most 40 per URL, and script findings, at most 20, from the median run.
- `src/analysis/components.ts` (`inventory-v1`). Each record has:
  - a stable `cmp-` id, pages, reach, families, occurrences, nodes and share of the page;
  - media, animations, resources with transfer, third-party hosts and examples;
  - plain-language **signals** and an identification confidence.

  There is **no score**. Evidence is matched in one of three ways:
  - `node-path`: the deepest occurrence that contains the element;
  - `tag-path`: element names only, accepted only when exactly one occurrence fits;
  - `script-in-component`: the script or iframe is loaded inside the component.

  Everything else is listed as **unattributed, with a reason**.
- `templatesForInstances` in `twig-debug.ts`: the innermost template wins.
- Output:
  - `<result>.components.csv`, with formula-safe cells;
  - a _Components_ section in the HTML report (tables plus `<details>`, no scripts, checked at 390 px);
  - a folded _Components worth a look_ list in the issue comment, with page text Markdown-escaped;
  - a compact entry in the site index;
  - `web-energy components <result.json> [--json] [--write]`;
  - `diagnose` prints the largest components and their templates.
- CI: a new `e2e-components` job. It installs fully, records in Firefox, runs Lighthouse in the runner's Chrome, and asserts that a Chrome finding lands on the component recorded in Firefox, matched by node path.
- Docs: `docs/component-inventory.md`, TDR-015, and notes in the README, methodology and limitations.

## Risks

- **DOM differences between Chromium and Firefox** (scripts, user-agent branches) can break path matches. Matches record their method; weak matches need a unique candidate; misses are listed. The new CI job is the real test of the common case.
- **Result size**: about 4–10 KB more per page. Instances are capped at 24 per page with 6 resources each; the index keeps 10 summaries.
- **More Lighthouse data kept per URL**: at most 60 entries from the median run.
- **The class-name fallback** may join unrelated markup or split a component. It is marked low confidence.

## Limitations

- Stylesheets, fonts and CSS background images are not tied to components.
- Bundled and global scripts stay unattributed, with that reason.
- Style recalculation and paint are page-level in Lighthouse and stay page-level here. Firefox CPU categories are page-level too; `diagnose` shows them for one page.
- Without `--lighthouse` the inventory is structure only, and says so.
- Twig association is development only. It is never used while benchmarking.

## How to validate

1. `npm test`: 552 tests, including `tests/components.test.ts`. They cover:
   - evidence parsing against a trimmed real Lighthouse 13 report (`tests/fixtures/lhr13-evidence.json`);
   - deepest-match and path-boundary rules;
   - the tag-path fallback and refusing ambiguous matches;
   - per-page evidence with queries kept (`?page=2` is another page);
   - script attribution and the unattributed reasons;
   - stable ids and order independence;
   - a check that no record carries an energy unit;
   - Twig innermost-template matching;
   - CSV formula neutralising;
   - report markup.
2. CI `e2e-components` on this PR: the cross-browser node-path match.
3. Locally, with Lighthouse installed: `node dist/cli/index.js crawl https://example.org --sample --max-pages 5 --lighthouse --lighthouse-top 2 --no-energy`, then open the HTML report's _Components_ section or `results/*.components.csv`.
4. On a development Drupal site with Twig debug on: `node dist/cli/index.js diagnose <url>` lists the largest components with "rendered by …".

## Update: findings and context

A second commit splits Lighthouse evidence into **findings** and **context**. Context is the LCP element, or the deepest node of a page whose DOM-size audit passes. It no longer counts as evidence of a problem, and the LCP element becomes a caution: keep that image eager. Every row of non-composited-animations, layout-shift and image audits stays a finding, as do long tasks, forced reflows, and failing script and DOM audits. This was found while building #34.

